course: dictionary
lesson: 690
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning. The word band is a good example. Read the words around it, because they tell you which meaning is right. «After a month of walks they find they both hate the same teacher's jokes and love the same band.» Here a band is a group of musicians, because you can love a band the way you love a pop group.
explain: «At night a driver sees a child in dark clothes; reflective bands shine the headlights straight back.» Here a band is a thin strip that goes round something. The clue is reflective: a strip that shines. Always ask: what word would fit here? Then try your meaning in the sentence to check it.
explain: «The Milky Way is our galaxy, and away from city lights you can see it as a pale band across the sky.» Here a band is a stripe, a long strip of colour or light. It is not a group of people and not something you wear. The context shows you the right meaning.
fact: Band is a noun, and it can also be a verb: to band together means to join as a group.
fact: «Band» can mean a group of musicians, such as a rock band or a school band.
fact: «Band» can mean a thin strip that goes round something, such as a rubber band or a hair band.
fact: «Band» can mean a stripe of colour or light, such as a band of cloud.
fact: «Band» can mean a group of people doing something together, such as a band of robbers.
exercise: «After a month of walks they find they both love the same band.» What does band mean here? | *A group of musicians who play together | A thin strip that goes round something | A rope used to tie up a boat | A stripe of colour across the sky
exercise: «The Milky Way is our galaxy, and away from city lights you can see it as a pale band across the sky.» What kind of band is this? | *A stripe of colour or light | A marching band | A hair band | A group of robbers
exercise: What does «band together» mean? | To stretch a rubber band | To cut a strip of cloth | To play a tune loudly | *To join as a group to do something
test: At school, pupils join the school band or orchestra. What does band mean in this sentence? | *A group of musicians who play together | A stripe of colour | A rubber band | A group of robbers
test: A rubber band can stretch and then spring back. What kind of band is this? | A band of outlaws | *A thin strip that goes round something | A stripe of colour | A group of people
exam: Read the sentence: «He put a band in his hair before the match.» Which meaning of band fits best, and why? | A stripe of colour, because it shows a range | *A thin strip that goes round something, because a hair band goes round the hair | A group of people who do something together | A group of musicians, because he plays in a band
